      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Digital health – defined broadly as the systematic application of information and communications technologies, computer science and data to support \ninformed decision-making by individuals, the health workforce and health systems, to strengthen resilience to disease and improve health and wellness \n(1) – is increasingly being applied as an essential enabler for health service delivery and accountability. Ministries of health have recognized the value \nof digital health as articulated within the World Health Assembly resolution (2) and the Global strategy on digital health (3). Likewise, donors",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines digital health and explains that guidelines are usually narrative, so translating them into digital systems is costly and inconsistent, and system content is often undocumented or proprietary. It introduces digital adaptation kits as a way to package WHO recommendations into standard, transparent requirements within the SMART Guidelines approach. It also explains digital tracking and decision support and the stages from paper to smart digital systems.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
